# Minimum LINE coverage for dupescan.
#
# Enforced by this repository's CI, which passes this number to
# `node --test-coverage-lines`. Node has had a coverage threshold built in since
# v22, so unlike the two Go modules this one needs no tool of ours -- but the
# number still lives in a file of its own, read by the Makefile, for the same
# reason qualityscan/.coverage-floor does: a threshold spelled twice is a
# threshold that can drift between the gate a developer runs and the gate that
# blocks a merge.
#
# WHAT IS MEASURED
#
# `node --experimental-test-coverage --test dupescan/dupescan.test.mjs`,
# which is the same suite the CI test job runs. Node merges V8 coverage from
# child processes, so the tests that drive the scanner as a command -- the only
# way to reach walk(), loadConfig(), parseArgs() and main(), none of which are
# exported -- count towards this figure.
#
# WHERE THE NUMBER CAME FROM
#
# 100.00% of lines measured on this branch, with the ranking, severity, CLI and
# reporting tests added: every line of dupescan.mjs is executed by the suite.
# Set to 99.5, which is about three lines of slack in a 770-line file.
#
# LINES ONLY, DELIBERATELY
#
# Node can also gate on branches and functions. Neither is used here:
#
#   branches   moves between 96.59% and 97.06% across runs of an unchanged
#              tree, because merged subprocess V8 coverage is not byte-stable.
#              A gate on it would fail intermittently with no code change, and a
#              gate that fails at random is one people learn to re-run rather
#              than read.
#   functions  reads 89.39%, but the figure is not comparable with the 75.00%
#              this file's suite scored before, because subprocess coverage
#              reports functions at a finer granularity than the in-process
#              import did. A floor on a denominator that changes with how the
#              tests are invoked measures the harness, not the code.
#
# Lines held identical across every run of both measurements, which is why it is
# the one carrying the threshold.
99.5
